    <h2 id="method">The method: evidence before action</h2>
    <p>
      Most bad outcomes on a production server come from acting before
      understanding. Rebooting a server that's in a bad state destroys the
      exact evidence you need to find the root cause. Killing a process
      because it's using a lot of CPU doesn't tell you why it started doing
      that, and the same failure often comes back an hour later.
    </p>
    <p>
      The method that works, regardless of what's actually broken, is
      always the same four steps:
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Confirm scope.</strong> Is this one host, one service, or a whole subnet? Don't fix a DNS problem as if it were a disk problem.</li>
      <li><strong>Collect evidence.</strong> Logs, resource usage, process state, and recent changes — before you touch anything.</li>
      <li><strong>Make the smallest safe change.</strong> Fix the specific thing the evidence points to, not everything that looks suspicious.</li>
      <li><strong>Verify and document.</strong> Confirm the fix actually worked, and write down what happened so the next person — or you, at 3 a.m. next month — doesn't start from zero.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>
      The rest of this guide applies that method to the failures that show
      up most often on real servers.
    </p>

    <h2 id="boot">A server won't boot or drops to emergency mode</h2>
    <p>
      Systemd drops you into emergency or rescue mode when something it
      considers essential — usually a filesystem mount — failed. The
      temptation is to reboot again and hope it fixes itself. It won't; the
      same failure will happen again.
    </p>

    <h3>Read what actually failed</h3>
    <pre><code>systemctl --failed
journalctl -xb
lsblk -f
cat /etc/fstab</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>systemctl --failed</code> lists every unit that didn't come up
      cleanly. <code>journalctl -xb</code> shows the boot log for the
      current boot with explanations attached to error codes.
      <code>lsblk -f</code> shows you what block devices and filesystems
      actually exist right now, which you then compare against
      <code>/etc/fstab</code> — the most common cause of an emergency-mode
      boot is an fstab entry pointing at a UUID or device that no longer
      exists, often after a disk was resized, cloned, or replaced.
    </p>

    <h3>Test the fix without rebooting first</h3>
    <pre><code>mount -a
systemctl daemon-reload</code></pre>
    <p>
      If <code>mount -a</code> succeeds with no errors, correct the fstab
      entry and only then continue the boot with <code>systemctl
      default</code>, or reboot to prove it's fixed for good.
    </p>

    <h2 id="cpu">CPU usage is pinned at 100%</h2>
    <p>
      Before touching anything, find out whether this is one runaway
      process, many processes competing for CPU, or the system spending
      its time waiting on I/O instead of computing — because
      <code>top</code>'s headline number doesn't tell you which.
    </p>

    <h3>Identify what kind of CPU problem this actually is</h3>
    <pre><code>uptime
top
ps -eo pid,ppid,user,%cpu,%mem,stat,etime,cmd --sort=-%cpu | head</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>uptime</code> gives you the 1/5/15-minute load average — compare
      it against <code>nproc</code> to know whether the load is actually
      high relative to how many CPUs the box has. In <code>top</code>, press
      <code>1</code> to see per-core usage; if only one core is saturated
      while others are idle, you're likely looking at a single-threaded
      process, not a systemic problem.
    </p>

    <h3>Investigate the process before touching it</h3>
    <p>
      Once you've identified the process, don't kill it yet. Check what it
      was doing and correlate with recent changes:
    </p>
    <pre><code>top -H -p &lt;PID&gt;
journalctl --since '-15 min'</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>top -H</code> shows individual threads inside that process,
      which is often the difference between "the whole application is
      slow" and "one worker thread is stuck in a loop." Only after you
      understand what's happening should you decide whether to restart the
      service — and if you do, capture a snapshot first
      (<code>ps</code> output, or a <code>perf</code>/<code>strace</code>
      capture if you have the tooling) so the next occurrence has more
      evidence to work with.
    </p>

    <h2 id="memory">The server is out of memory or swapping</h2>
    <p>
      Linux deliberately uses "free" RAM for filesystem cache, so the
      <code>free</code> column in <code>free -h</code> almost always looks
      low — that's normal, not a problem. What actually matters is the
      <code>available</code> column and whether the kernel is actively
      swapping.
    </p>

    <h3>Check whether this is real memory pressure</h3>
    <pre><code>free -h
vmstat 1
ps -eo pid,user,%mem,rss,vsz,cmd --sort=-rss | head</code></pre>
    <p>
      In <code>vmstat 1</code>, watch the <code>si</code> and
      <code>so</code> columns (swap in/swap out). If they're consistently
      non-zero while the application feels slow, the system is under real
      memory pressure. If swap usage is high but <code>si</code>/<code>so</code>
      are near zero, that swap is just old, inactive data sitting there —
      not an active problem.
    </p>

    <h3>Check whether the kernel already killed something</h3>
    <p>
      If a process was killed, the kernel log tells you exactly which one
      and why:
    </p>
    <pre><code>journalctl -k | grep -i -E 'oom|out of memory'</code></pre>
    <p>
      Use that to identify the actual memory consumer from the
      <code>ps</code> output above before deciding whether to add memory,
      fix a leak, or set a resource limit so a single process can't take
      the whole host down again.
    </p>

    <h2 id="disk">A filesystem is full</h2>
    <p>
      Protect service stability first — a full root filesystem can stop
      logging, break package operations, and crash applications that can't
      write temp files.
    </p>

    <h3>Find which filesystem is actually full</h3>
    <pre><code>df -hT
df -i</code></pre>
    <p>
      Check inodes as well as blocks — a filesystem can report plenty of
      free space while still refusing to create new files because it ran
      out of inodes, usually from a huge number of tiny files.
    </p>

    <h3>Find what's actually consuming the space</h3>
    <pre><code>du -xhd1 / | sort -h
find / -xdev -type f -size +1G -ls</code></pre>
    <p>
      These two commands cover both cases: <code>du</code> finds the
      largest directories, <code>find</code> finds individual large files
      that a directory-level summary can hide.
    </p>

    <h3>Check for a deleted-but-still-open file</h3>
    <p>
      If <code>du</code> and <code>df</code> disagree — <code>df</code>
      says full but <code>du</code> can't account for the space — the
      classic cause is a deleted file that's still open by a running
      process. The directory entry is gone so <code>du</code> can't see
      it, but the blocks stay allocated until the process closes the file.
      Find it with:
    </p>
    <pre><code>lsof +L1</code></pre>
    <p>
      That lists open files with a link count of zero — deleted-but-open
      files. Restarting the owning service (or reopening its log file, for
      applications that support that) releases the space immediately.
    </p>

    <h2 id="dfhang"><code>df -h</code> hangs but the server isn't frozen</h2>
    <p>
      You run <code>df -h</code> on a production server and it just sits
      there. The instinct is to assume the whole server has locked up.
      Usually it hasn't — a much more common cause is one unresponsive
      mounted filesystem, especially an NFS mount, blocking the one
      command that needs to talk to it.
    </p>

    <h3>Why df -h can hang</h3>
    <p>
      <code>df</code> retrieves usage statistics for every mounted
      filesystem. If a remote mount is unavailable or responding very
      slowly, that one request can block while the kernel waits or
      retries — and because <code>df</code> checks filesystems in order,
      the whole command appears frozen even though every other filesystem
      on the box is fine.
    </p>
    <pre><code>df -h   # command waits / appears hung</code></pre>

    <h3>Don't immediately reboot the server</h3>
    <p>
      Open a <em>different</em> SSH or console session and troubleshoot
      the mounted filesystems from there — the original session may stay
      blocked, but that doesn't mean the server is unreachable. A reboot
      can hide the symptom temporarily while creating unnecessary
      production impact, and it destroys the evidence you'd need to find
      the actual cause.
    </p>

    <h3>Identify NFS mounts</h3>
    <pre><code>findmnt -t nfs,nfs4</code></pre>
    <p>Alternative checks if <code>findmnt</code> isn't available:</p>
    <pre><code>mount -t nfs,nfs4
cat /proc/mounts | grep -E ' nfs| nfs4'</code></pre>

    <h3>Check the NFS server</h3>
    <p>Assume the client has a mount similar to:</p>
    <pre><code>192.168.1.190:/data /mnt/data</code></pre>
    <p>Test basic IP connectivity first:</p>
    <pre><code>ping -c 4 192.168.1.190</code></pre>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Important:</strong> a successful ping only proves basic
      IP/ICMP reachability. It does not prove the NFS service itself is
      healthy — plenty of hung NFS mounts sit behind a server that
      answers ping just fine.
    </div>

    <h3>Test the NFS port</h3>
    <p>For NFSv4, verify TCP port 2049 is actually reachable:</p>
    <pre><code>nc -zv -w 3 192.168.1.190 2049</code></pre>

    <h3>Check NFS services on the NFS server</h3>
    <pre><code>systemctl status nfs-server
ss -lntp | grep :2049</code></pre>
    <p>For environments still using NFSv3/RPC services:</p>
    <pre><code>rpcinfo -p</code></pre>
    <p>
      If the NFS service is unexpectedly stopped, investigate why before
      restarting it. If restarting is appropriate under your change or
      incident procedure:
    </p>
    <pre><code>systemctl start nfs-server
systemctl status nfs-server</code></pre>

    <h3>Check exports</h3>
    <p>From the client, where applicable:</p>
    <pre><code>showmount -e 192.168.1.190</code></pre>
    <p>On the NFS server itself:</p>
    <pre><code>exportfs -v</code></pre>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Technical note:</strong> a failed <code>showmount -e</code>
      does not always prove <code>nfs-server</code> is stopped.
      <code>showmount</code> relies on RPC/mountd behavior and is
      especially relevant to NFSv3 — NFSv4-only configurations can behave
      differently.
    </div>

    <h3>Check kernel logs</h3>
    <pre><code>dmesg -T | tail -100
journalctl -k --since "30 minutes ago"</code></pre>
    <p>Typical NFS-related messages to look for:</p>
    <pre><code>nfs: server 192.168.1.190 not responding
nfs: server 192.168.1.190 OK</code></pre>

    <h3>Production troubleshooting workflow</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Check</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Open another session</td><td>Avoid interrupting the blocked <code>df</code> process/session.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td><code>findmnt -t nfs,nfs4</code></td><td>Identify remote NFS mounts.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Check kernel logs</td><td>Look for NFS timeout/not-responding messages.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Ping the NFS server</td><td>Verify basic network reachability.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Test TCP 2049</td><td>Verify NFS service connectivity, especially NFSv4.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td><code>systemctl status nfs-server</code></td><td>Check NFS server-side service state.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td><code>exportfs -v</code> / RPC checks</td><td>Validate exports and relevant RPC services.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>8</td><td>Fix root cause and retest</td><td>Confirm <code>df -h</code> returns normally.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Interview-ready answer</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Question:</strong> Why can <code>df -h</code> hang on Linux,
      and how would you troubleshoot it?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer:</strong> <code>df -h</code> can hang when it tries
      to retrieve filesystem statistics from an unresponsive mounted
      filesystem — a common production cause is a stale or unavailable
      NFS mount. I wouldn't immediately reboot the server. I'd open
      another session, identify NFS mounts with <code>findmnt</code>,
      check kernel logs for NFS timeout messages, verify connectivity to
      the NFS server and TCP port 2049, and check the NFS services and
      exports on the server. After identifying and fixing the affected
      remote filesystem, I'd rerun <code>df -h</code> and confirm normal
      behavior.
    </div>

    <h3>Quick troubleshooting map</h3>
    <div class="flow-map">
      df -h hangs → Check mounted filesystems → Remote / NFS mount? →
      Check kernel logs → Check NFS server connectivity → Check TCP 2049 /
      RPC → Check nfs-server + exports → Fix root cause → Retest df -h
    </div>

    <h3>Key production lesson</h3>
    <p>
      A hanging <code>df -h</code> is a symptom, not a diagnosis. NFS is a
      common cause, but any unresponsive mounted filesystem can create
      similar behavior. Troubleshoot the mount, the network, the
      filesystem service, and the kernel messages before considering
      disruptive actions like a reboot.
    </p>

    <h2 id="hardlink">A hard link fails with "Invalid cross-device link"</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Interview question:</strong> can we create a hard link
      across different partitions or filesystems?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer: no.</strong> A hard link can only refer to an inode
      within the <em>same</em> filesystem. A symbolic (soft) link stores
      a pathname instead, so it can point to a target on another mounted
      filesystem — see the next section for that side of it.
    </div>

    <h3>The production scenario</h3>
    <p>
      <code>/data</code> is mounted from one filesystem and
      <code>/splunklog</code> from another:
    </p>
    <pre><code>/dev/sdb1 -&gt; /data
/dev/sdb2 -&gt; /splunklog</code></pre>
    <p>You try to hard-link a file from one to the other:</p>
    <pre><code>ln /data/server.xml /splunklog/server.xml</code></pre>
    <p>It fails immediately:</p>
    <pre><code>ln: failed to create hard link '/splunklog/server.xml' =&gt; '/data/server.xml': Invalid cross-device link</code></pre>

    <h3>Why the hard link fails</h3>
    <p>
      A hard link is just another directory entry pointing at the same
      inode as an existing filename — and inode numbers are only
      meaningful within a single filesystem. A directory entry on a
      different filesystem has no way to directly reference an inode that
      belongs to a different one, so the kernel rejects the operation,
      commonly surfaced as <code>EXDEV</code> / "Invalid cross-device
      link." This isn't a permissions or ownership problem — no amount of
      <code>chmod</code> or <code>chown</code> fixes it, because the
      restriction is structural, not access-based.
    </p>

    <h3>Prove it with inode numbers</h3>
    <p>Create a file and a hard link on the <em>same</em> filesystem:</p>
    <pre><code>echo 'Linux production' &gt; /data/server.xml
ln /data/server.xml /data/server-hard.xml
ls -li /data/server.xml /data/server-hard.xml</code></pre>
    <p>Both names show the same inode number. You can also compare with:</p>
    <pre><code>stat /data/server.xml
stat /data/server-hard.xml</code></pre>

    <h3>The soft-link alternative</h3>
    <p>
      A symbolic link doesn't reference an inode directly — it stores the
      target's pathname as text, so it can point anywhere, including
      across filesystems:
    </p>
    <pre><code>ln -s /data/server.xml /splunklog/server-soft.xml
ls -l /splunklog/server-soft.xml</code></pre>
    <pre><code>/splunklog/server-soft.xml -&gt; /data/server.xml</code></pre>

    <h3>Hard link vs. soft link</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Hard link</th><th>Soft / symbolic link</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>What it refers to</td><td>Same underlying inode</td><td>Target pathname</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Cross filesystem?</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Own inode?</td><td>Directory entry refers to target inode</td><td>Yes — the symlink itself has an inode</td></tr>
          <tr><td>If original name is deleted</td><td>Data stays accessible through the other hard link</td><td>Symlink becomes dangling if the target path no longer resolves</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can normally link directories?</td><td>Generally prohibited for users</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Creation</td><td><code>ln source link</code></td><td><code>ln -s target link</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>What happens if the original file is deleted?</h3>
    <p>
      <strong>Hard link:</strong> deleting one filename does not delete
      the file's data while another hard link still references the
      inode. The data is only removed once the link count reaches zero
      <em>and</em> no process still has the file open:
    </p>
    <pre><code><mark class="danger">rm</mark> /data/server.xml
cat /data/server-hard.xml   # still works</code></pre>
    <p>
      <strong>Soft link:</strong> if the target pathname is removed and
      nothing replaces it at that path, the symbolic link remains but
      becomes a dangling/broken link.
    </p>

    <h3>Check link count and filesystem boundaries</h3>
    <pre><code>ls -li /data/server.xml
stat /data/server.xml
findmnt -T /data/server.xml
findmnt -T /splunklog
df -T /data /splunklog</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>findmnt -T</code> is particularly useful here because
      different mount points don't always mean different filesystems,
      while different block devices often do. The actual restriction is
      the filesystem boundary — not the directory name or the partition
      label.
    </p>

    <h3>Scenario-based interview questions</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Short answer</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Can a hard link cross filesystems?</td><td>No — it must reference an inode in the same filesystem.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can a symbolic link cross filesystems?</td><td>Yes — it stores a target pathname.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What happens if one hard-link name is deleted?</td><td>The other hard links keep accessing the same inode/data.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What happens if a symlink target is deleted?</td><td>The symlink normally becomes dangling.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How do you verify two names are hard links to the same file?</td><td>Compare inode numbers with <code>ls -li</code> or <code>stat</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What error may appear for a cross-filesystem hard link?</td><td><code>Invalid cross-device link</code> (<code>EXDEV</code>).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Interview-ready answer</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Question:</strong> Can we create a hard link across
      partitions or filesystems?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer:</strong> No, not when the source and destination
      are on different filesystems. A hard link creates another directory
      entry for the same inode, and an inode belongs to its filesystem —
      it's not a portable reference. If I try
      <code>ln /data/server.xml /splunklog/server.xml</code> where the
      two paths are on different filesystems, Linux returns an "Invalid
      cross-device link" error. A symbolic link can cross filesystem
      boundaries because it stores the target path rather than directly
      referencing the target inode. Also, if one hard-link filename is
      deleted, the file stays accessible through the other hard-link
      names until the final link is removed.
    </div>

    <h3>Key lesson</h3>
    <p>
      Don't stop at memorizing the feature table. Be ready to explain the
      inode relationship, demonstrate the cross-filesystem failure,
      interpret the error, verify inode numbers, and explain what happens
      when a linked filename or a symlink target gets removed. The
      hard-link restriction is across <em>filesystems</em>, which is
      often — but not always — the same thing as across partitions.
    </p>

    <h2 id="softlink">Can a soft link cross filesystems?</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Interview question:</strong> can a soft (symbolic) link
      point to a target on a different filesystem or partition?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer: yes.</strong> A symbolic link stores the target's
      <em>pathname</em>, not its inode — so it can point anywhere that
      pathname resolves, including across a filesystem boundary. That's
      the opposite of a hard link, which references an inode directly and
      therefore cannot cross filesystems (see the previous section).
    </div>

    <h3>The scenario</h3>
    <p>
      Assume the original configuration file is on the root filesystem
      and <code>/boot</code> is a separate one:
    </p>
    <pre><code>/dev/sda3 -&gt; /
/dev/sda2 -&gt; /boot</code></pre>
    <p>The original file:</p>
    <pre><code>/config.conf</code></pre>
    <p>Create a symbolic link under the separate <code>/boot</code> filesystem:</p>
    <pre><code>ln -s /config.conf /boot/config.conf</code></pre>
    <p>Verify it:</p>
    <pre><code>ls -l /boot/config.conf
readlink /boot/config.conf
readlink -f /boot/config.conf</code></pre>

    <h3>Why it works across filesystems</h3>
    <p>
      A symbolic link is a special file whose only content is the target
      pathname. When a process opens the symlink, Linux resolves that
      pathname to the target at access time. Because pathname resolution
      can cross mount points, the symlink can point from one filesystem
      to a completely different one — it never needs to reference the
      target's inode number directly, which is exactly the restriction
      that blocks a hard link from doing the same thing.
    </p>

    <h3>Verify the filesystem boundary</h3>
    <p>Use <code>findmnt</code> or <code>df</code> to confirm the source and destination really are on different filesystems:</p>
    <pre><code>findmnt -T /config.conf
findmnt -T /boot/config.conf
df -T / /boot</code></pre>

    <h3>Production-style example</h3>
    <p>
      A common layout places application software under
      <code>/opt</code>, application data under <code>/data</code>, and
      selected configuration entry points under <code>/etc</code>.
      Symbolic links are useful when software expects a file at one
      pathname while the maintained file actually lives elsewhere:
    </p>
    <pre><code>ln -s /opt/myapp/conf/app.conf /etc/myapp.conf
ls -l /etc/myapp.conf</code></pre>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Production caution:</strong> don't create symlinks in
      system configuration paths without checking application
      expectations, package-management behavior, permissions, SELinux
      policy, backup processes, and your change-control requirements —
      a symlink in the wrong place can quietly redirect a service to the
      wrong file, or confuse a package manager that expects a real file
      there.
    </div>

    <h3>What if the original target is deleted?</h3>
    <p>
      If the target file is deleted or moved and nothing exists at the
      stored target pathname, the symbolic link remains but becomes a
      dangling (broken) symlink:
    </p>
    <pre><code><mark class="danger">rm</mark> /config.conf
ls -l /boot/config.conf     # the link entry still exists
cat /boot/config.conf       # but reading it now fails</code></pre>
    <p>
      The link itself isn't removed — only access through it fails. If a
      new file is later created at the same target pathname, the symlink
      resolves again without needing to be recreated.
    </p>

    <h3>Soft link vs. hard link — interview comparison</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Soft / symbolic link</th><th>Hard link</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Cross filesystem?</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Reference</td><td>Stores target pathname</td><td>Same underlying inode</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Own inode</td><td>Yes</td><td>Both names refer to the target inode</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Target deleted</td><td>Usually becomes dangling</td><td>Other hard-link names still access the data</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can link directories?</td><td>Yes</td><td>Normally prohibited for ordinary users</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Create</td><td><code>ln -s target link</code></td><td><code>ln source link</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Scenario-based interview questions</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Can a soft link cross filesystems?</td><td>Yes — it stores a pathname and can resolve across mount points.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can a hard link cross filesystems?</td><td>No — it must reference the same inode within one filesystem.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What happens when a symlink target is deleted?</td><td>The symlink normally becomes dangling/broken.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How do you see where a symlink points?</td><td>Use <code>ls -l</code> or <code>readlink</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How do you resolve the final target path?</td><td>Use <code>readlink -f</code> when the path can be resolved.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>How do you prove paths are on different filesystems?</td><td>Use <code>findmnt -T</code> or <code>df -T</code>.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Interview-ready answer</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Question:</strong> Can you create a soft link across
      partitions or filesystems?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer:</strong> Yes. A symbolic link can point across
      filesystem boundaries because it stores the pathname of the target
      rather than directly referencing the target inode. For example, if
      the source file is on the root filesystem and <code>/boot</code> is
      a separate filesystem, I can create
      <code>ln -s /config.conf /boot/config.conf</code>. I'd verify the
      filesystems with <code>findmnt -T</code> and the link itself with
      <code>ls -l</code> or <code>readlink</code>. If the original target
      is deleted or moved, the symbolic link normally becomes dangling
      until the target pathname exists again.
    </div>

    <h3>Key lesson</h3>
    <p>
      For Linux, AWS, and DevOps interviews, understand the behavior
      rather than memorizing only the definitions: symbolic links can
      cross filesystem boundaries, hard links cannot, and a symlink's
      usefulness depends entirely on its stored target pathname
      continuing to resolve.
    </p>

    <h2 id="service">A systemd service won't start</h2>
    <p>
      Work from evidence outward: the log first, then the configuration,
      then the command itself.
    </p>

    <h3>Read the status and journal first</h3>
    <p>The exit code and the last few log lines almost always point straight at the cause:</p>
    <pre><code>systemctl status &lt;service&gt; --no-pager -l
journalctl -u &lt;service&gt; -b --no-pager</code></pre>

    <h3>Check exactly how the unit is configured</h3>
    <p>If the reason isn't obvious from the logs, check exactly how the unit is configured to start, and as which user:</p>
    <pre><code>systemctl cat &lt;service&gt;
systemctl show &lt;service&gt; -p User -p Group -p ExecStart</code></pre>

    <h3>Run the ExecStart command by hand</h3>
    <p>
      A large share of "it just won't start" cases come down to running
      the exact <code>ExecStart</code> command by hand, as the configured
      user, to see the real error instead of systemd's generic failure
      message:
    </p>
    <pre><code>sudo -u &lt;user&gt; &lt;the ExecStart command&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      If you edited the unit file itself, remember that
      <code>systemctl daemon-reload</code> is required before systemd will
      pick up the change — a very common reason a fix "doesn't work" even
      though the file is correct.
    </p>

    <h2 id="llcommand">A working command like <code>ll</code> suddenly stops working</h2>
    <p>
      You're in <code>/data</code>. <code>pwd</code> confirms it. Then you
      run <code>ll</code> and get a "No such file or directory"-type error
      — on a command that was working an hour ago, in a directory that
      definitely exists. The instinct is to assume something is wrong with
      the directory or the shell itself. It usually isn't.
    </p>

    <h3>What's really happening</h3>
    <p>
      <code>ll</code> is almost never a standalone Linux binary. On most
      RHEL-family systems it's a shell alias, commonly pointing to
      <code>ls -l --color=auto</code>. That alias still has to resolve to
      the real <code>ls</code> executable, normally at
      <code>/usr/bin/ls</code>. If the shell's <code>PATH</code> variable
      is empty, corrupted, or missing the standard directories, the shell
      can no longer <em>find</em> <code>ls</code> to run it — so a command
      or alias that worked fine a moment ago suddenly appears broken, with
      an error that looks like a filesystem problem but isn't one.
    </p>

    <h3>First checks — don't restart the server</h3>
    <p>Confirm where you are, what <code>ll</code> actually resolves to, and whether <code>PATH</code> looks sane, in that order:</p>
    <pre><code>pwd
type ll
alias ll
echo "$PATH"</code></pre>
    <p>
      If <code>echo "$PATH"</code> comes back blank, or standard
      directories like <code>/usr/bin</code> and <code>/bin</code> are
      missing from it, <code>PATH</code> is almost certainly the problem —
      not the directory, not the alias definition itself.
    </p>

    <h3>Temporary recovery</h3>
    <p>Restore a safe, standard <code>PATH</code> for the current shell session:</p>
    <pre><code>export PATH=/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin</code></pre>
    <p>Verify it, then retry the command:</p>
    <pre><code>echo "$PATH"
command -v ls
ll</code></pre>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>A common misconception:</strong> just running
      <code>export PATH</code> on its own does not restore anything if
      <code>PATH</code> is already empty — <code>export</code> only marks
      an existing variable for the environment, it doesn't invent a value.
      You have to explicitly assign the directories, as above, unless a
      valid value already existed but simply hadn't been exported yet.
    </div>

    <h3>Bypass PATH to confirm the diagnosis</h3>
    <p>Before changing anything, call the real executable by its absolute path, bypassing <code>PATH</code> entirely:</p>
    <pre><code>/usr/bin/ls -l /data</code></pre>
    <p>
      If that works while <code>ls</code> or <code>ll</code> doesn't, you've
      confirmed it's a <code>PATH</code> or shell-configuration problem,
      not a filesystem problem — you can now fix it with confidence instead
      of guessing.
    </p>

    <h3>Find out why PATH changed</h3>
    <p>
      Restoring the command isn't the end of the job — a production fix
      means finding out why it broke. Check shell startup files and the
      system-wide profile configuration for accidental overwrites:
    </p>
    <pre><code>grep -n 'PATH' ~/.bash_profile ~/.bashrc ~/.profile 2&gt;/dev/null
grep -R "PATH=" /etc/profile /etc/profile.d/ 2&gt;/dev/null</code></pre>
    <p>
      Look for a line like <code>PATH=/some/custom/path</code> that
      overwrites the existing value outright instead of extending it. When
      a script or profile file needs to add a directory, it should always
      preserve the current value:
    </p>
    <pre><code>export PATH="$PATH:/opt/myapp/bin"</code></pre>

    <h3>Production troubleshooting flow</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Command / action</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td><code>pwd</code></td><td>Confirm the current directory.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td><code>type ll</code> / <code>alias ll</code></td><td>Determine whether <code>ll</code> is an alias and what it executes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td><code>echo "$PATH"</code></td><td>Check whether command-search paths are missing.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td><code>/usr/bin/ls -l</code></td><td>Bypass PATH and test the actual executable.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td><code>export PATH=...</code></td><td>Temporarily restore standard command paths.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td><code>command -v ls</code></td><td>Confirm the shell can now locate <code>ls</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Check <code>.bashrc</code> / <code>.bash_profile</code> / <code>/etc/profile.d</code></td><td>Identify the root cause and make the correct persistent fix.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Interview-style answer</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Question:</strong> The <code>ll</code> command suddenly
      stopped working on a production Linux server. What would you do?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer:</strong> I wouldn't restart the server. First I'd
      check whether <code>ll</code> is an alias with <code>type ll</code>
      or <code>alias ll</code>, then inspect <code>$PATH</code>. I'd test
      the underlying command with an absolute path like
      <code>/usr/bin/ls -l</code> — if that works, it confirms a
      <code>PATH</code> or shell-configuration issue, not a filesystem
      one. I'd restore a valid <code>PATH</code>, verify the command
      works, then check the shell profile files to find out why
      <code>PATH</code> was overwritten or cleared, so the same fix
      doesn't have to be repeated by hand next time.
    </div>

    <h3>Key lesson</h3>
    <p>
      A reboot can make a shell-environment problem disappear temporarily,
      but it doesn't tell you why it happened — and the same broken
      profile file will do it again on the next login or the next server.
      Checking the alias, <code>PATH</code>, the absolute command path,
      and the profile files gets you the actual diagnosis and a fix that
      stays fixed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="network">The server has network connectivity problems</h2>
    <p>
      Separate "can't route to it" from "DNS won't resolve it" from "can
      route and resolve, but nothing is listening" — they have completely
      different fixes, and testing them out of order wastes time.
    </p>

    <h3>Separate routing, DNS, and listening</h3>
    <pre><code>ping -c 3 &lt;gateway&gt;
ping -c 3 1.1.1.1
dig example.com
ss -lntp</code></pre>
    <p>
      If you can ping an IP address but not a hostname, it's DNS, not
      routing — check the resolver configuration
      (<code>resolvectl status</code>) rather than touching routes. If a
      service is supposedly running but nothing external can reach it,
      confirm it's actually listening on the interface you expect with
      <code>ss -lntp</code> before assuming it's a firewall problem — a
      service bound only to <code>127.0.0.1</code> will never accept
      external connections no matter what the firewall allows.
    </p>

    <h3>Isolate the host from the network path</h3>
    <p>
      Once the process is confirmed listening on the right address, test
      locally first, then from a remote client:
    </p>
    <pre><code>curl -v http://127.0.0.1:&lt;port&gt;/
nc -vz &lt;server&gt; &lt;port&gt;</code></pre>
    <p>
      If the local <code>curl</code> works but the remote <code>nc</code>
      doesn't, the service itself is healthy — the problem is between the
      host and the client: a firewall rule, a security group, or a routing
      hop in between.
    </p>

    <h2 id="sscommand">How Do You Check Whether a Service Port Is Listening?</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Recommended answer:</strong> use <code>ss</code>, for
      example <code>ss -lntp</code>. The <code>ss</code> utility displays
      socket information and is the standard modern tool available on
      most current Linux distributions.
    </div>

    <h3>Core command</h3>
    <p>Display listening TCP sockets numerically, with the owning process:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo ss -lntp</code></pre>
    <p>Look specifically for SSH/sshd:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo ss -lntp | grep sshd</code></pre>
    <p>A more reliable, port-specific check for the standard SSH port:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo ss -lntp '( sport = :22 )'</code></pre>

    <h3>What do the options mean?</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Option</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>-l</code></td><td>Show listening sockets only.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>-n</code></td><td>Show numeric addresses and port numbers; avoid service-name resolution.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>-t</code></td><td>Show TCP sockets.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>-p</code></td><td>Show process information associated with the socket (permissions may require root).</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Example SSH output</h3>
    <pre><code>sudo ss -lntp | grep ':22'</code></pre>
    <p>Example output:</p>
    <pre><code>LISTEN 0 128 0.0.0.0:22 0.0.0.0:* users:(("sshd",pid=1120,fd=3))
LISTEN 0 128 [::]:22 [::]:* users:(("sshd",pid=1120,fd=4))</code></pre>
    <p>
      The first line is an IPv4 wildcard listener, the second an IPv6
      wildcard listener. <code>LISTEN</code> confirms a local TCP socket
      is in the listening state, and the process field identifies the
      owner when permissions allow it.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Important production clarification:</strong> a
      <code>LISTEN</code> socket does not by itself prove that remote
      clients can reach the port. It only proves a process is listening
      locally on a particular address/port. Remote access can still be
      blocked by the bind address, host firewall, cloud security group,
      network ACL, routing, a load balancer, or another network control.
      For example, a listener on <code>127.0.0.1:8080</code> is only
      reachable through the loopback interface, whereas one bound to
      <code>0.0.0.0:8080</code> is listening on every IPv4 interface,
      subject to firewall and network policy.
    </div>

    <h3>Local listening check vs. remote connectivity test</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Command / method</th><th>What it proves</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Is the service listening locally?</td><td><code>ss -lntp</code></td><td>A local TCP socket is in <code>LISTEN</code> state.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Which process owns the port?</td><td><code>sudo ss -lntp</code></td><td>Displays process/PID where permitted.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Can another host reach the port?</td><td><code>nc -zv server port</code></td><td>Tests network connectivity to that TCP port.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Is the service itself healthy?</td><td>Protocol-specific test</td><td>Requires an application-level check, e.g. <code>curl</code> for HTTP.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Useful production commands</h3>
    <pre><code># All listening TCP ports
sudo ss -lntp

# All listening TCP and UDP sockets
sudo ss -lntup

# Check port 22
sudo ss -lntp | grep ':22'

# Check port 443
sudo ss -lntp | grep ':443'

# Show established TCP sessions
ss -nt state established

# Test a port remotely
nc -zv -w 3 server.example.com 443</code></pre>

    <h3>What about netstat?</h3>
    <p>
      <code>netstat</code> isn't necessarily "wrong" — it belongs to the
      older <code>net-tools</code> package and isn't installed by default
      on many modern distributions. For current Linux environments,
      <code>ss</code> is generally the preferred socket-inspection
      utility. In an interview, saying that is more accurate than simply
      calling <code>netstat</code> invalid.
    </p>

    <h3>Production troubleshooting workflow</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Check</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Check service state: <code>systemctl status &lt;service&gt;</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Check the listener: <code>sudo ss -lntp</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Confirm the bind address and expected port.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>Identify the owning process/PID.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>Check the host firewall/firewalld/nftables if remote access fails.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Test from the remote client with <code>nc</code>/<code>curl</code> or the appropriate protocol.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Check routing, security groups/ACLs, load balancers, and application logs as applicable.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Interview-ready answer</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Question:</strong> How will you check whether a service
      port is listening on Linux?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer:</strong> I normally use <code>ss -lntp</code>.
      <code>-l</code> shows listening sockets, <code>-n</code> keeps
      addresses and ports numeric, <code>-t</code> selects TCP, and
      <code>-p</code> shows the owning process when I have sufficient
      permissions. For example, I'd use
      <code>sudo ss -lntp | grep ':22'</code> to check SSH. If I see a
      TCP socket in <code>LISTEN</code> state, I know a local process is
      listening on that address and port — but I wouldn't conclude the
      port is remotely reachable until I also check the bind address,
      firewall/network controls, and test connectivity from the client
      side.
    </div>

    <h3>Key lesson</h3>
    <p>
      For production troubleshooting, keep three questions separate: is a
      process listening locally? Can the network reach it? And is the
      application actually healthy? <code>ss</code> answers the first
      question extremely well — additional network and application tests
      answer the other two.
    </p>

    <h2 id="rootpw">Locked out: a lost or forgotten root password</h2>
    <p>
      This isn't a "diagnose the cause" scenario like the ones above — it's
      a known, repeatable recovery procedure. It requires direct console
      access to the machine (physical, KVM/iLO/iDRAC, or your hypervisor's
      console view). <strong>You cannot do this over SSH</strong> — if you
      could SSH in, you wouldn't need to reset the password. Every version
      below follows the same shape: interrupt GRUB, drop into a minimal
      shell before the normal boot finishes, remount root read-write, set
      the password, flag SELinux for a relabel, then let the boot finish.
      Click a version below for the exact steps.
    </p>

    <div class="qa-accordion">
      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>RHEL 8 and RHEL 9: step-by-step recovery</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Reboot the machine and, at the boot menu, use the arrow keys to select the kernel entry, then press <code>e</code> to edit it — this is the exact screen you'll see:</p>
          <div class="mockup">
            <div class="mockup-bar">RHEL 8 boot menu</div>
            <pre class="mockup-body">Red Hat Enterprise Linux (4.18.0-80.11.2.el8_0.x86_64) 8.0 (Ootpa)
Red Hat Enterprise Linux (4.18.0-80.el8.x86_64) 8.0 (Ootpa)
Red Hat Enterprise Linux (0-rescue-87b117ed44b6449b9386cbb97b322517) 8.0

Use the &uarr; and &darr; keys to change the selection.
Press 'e' to edit the selected item, or 'c' for a command prompt.</pre>
          </div>
          <p>On the edit screen, find the line beginning with <code>linux</code> (it also carries a <code>kernel=</code>-style path), go to the end of that line, and type <code>rd.break</code>:</p>
          <div class="mockup">
            <div class="mockup-bar">append kernel parameter</div>
            <pre class="mockup-body">linux ($root)/vmlinuz-4.18.0-80.11.2.el8_0.x86_64 root=/dev/mapper/rhel-root ro
   crashkernel=auto resume=/dev/mapper/rhel-swap rd.lvm.lv=rhel/root rd.lvm.lv=
   rhel/swap rhgb quiet <mark>rd.break</mark>
initrd  ($root)/initramfs-4.18.0-80.11.2.el8_0.x86_64.img $tuned_initrd

Press Ctrl-x to start, Ctrl-c for a command prompt or Escape to
discard edits and return to the menu.</pre>
          </div>
          <p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>X</kbd> to boot with that change. You land in emergency mode at a <code>switch_root:/#</code> prompt, with the real root filesystem mounted read-only under <code>/sysroot</code>. Confirm that, remount it read-write, and confirm again — watch <code>ro</code> become <code>rw</code> in the output:</p>
          <div class="mockup">
            <div class="mockup-bar">emergency mode shell</div>
            <pre class="mockup-body">Entering emergency mode. Exit the shell to continue.
Type "journalctl" to view system logs.

switch_root:/# mount | grep sysroot
/dev/mapper/rhel-root on /sysroot type xfs (<mark>ro</mark>,relatime,attr2,inode64,noquota)

switch_root:/# mount -o remount,rw /sysroot/
switch_root:/# mount | grep sysroot
/dev/mapper/rhel-root on /sysroot type xfs (<mark>rw</mark>,relatime,attr2,inode64,noquota)</pre>
          </div>
          <p><code>chroot</code> into it so the password change applies to the real filesystem, then run <code>passwd</code> — provide the new password and confirm it when prompted:</p>
          <div class="mockup">
            <div class="mockup-bar">reset the password</div>
            <pre class="mockup-body">switch_root:/# chroot /sysroot
sh-4.4# passwd
Changing password for user root.
New password:
Retype new password:
passwd: all authentication tokens updated successfully.
sh-4.4#</pre>
          </div>
          <p>Flag SELinux so it relabels the filesystem on next boot instead of leaving mismatched contexts on the password file, then exit the chroot twice and reboot:</p>
          <pre><code>touch /.autorelabel
exit
exit
reboot</code></pre>
          <p>The relabel adds time to this one boot — that's expected. Log in with the new password once it completes.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>RHEL 10: step-by-step recovery</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p><strong>Method A — try <code>rd.break</code> first</strong>, the same as RHEL 8/9 above. If your minor release still supports it, the same confirm-remount-<code>chroot</code>-<code>passwd</code>-<code>autorelabel</code> sequence applies unchanged.</p>
          <p><strong>Method B — if <code>rd.break</code> doesn't drop you into a shell</strong>, edit the GRUB boot line again, but this time change <code>ro</code> to <code>rw</code> and append <code>init=/bin/bash</code> instead:</p>
          <pre><code>... root=/dev/mapper/rhel-root <mark>rw</mark> crashkernel=auto <mark>init=/bin/bash</mark></code></pre>
          <p>Press <kbd>Ctrl</kbd> + <kbd>X</kbd>. Because <code>init=/bin/bash</code> replaces systemd's init entirely, you land straight in a root shell with the filesystem already mounted read-write — no chroot needed. If it isn't, remount it: <code>mount -o remount,rw /</code>. Then:</p>
          <pre><code>passwd
touch /.autorelabel</code></pre>
          <p>Since you replaced init rather than using a rescue shell, hand off to the real init process instead of forcing a power cycle:</p>
          <pre><code>exec /sbin/init</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>RHEL 10 note:</strong> confirm which method your exact minor
      release expects against Red Hat's own documentation before relying
      on this in production — public write-ups disagree with each other on
      this specific point, and recovery procedures are exactly the kind of
      detail that gets refined between minor releases.
    </div>

    <h3>Verify it actually worked</h3>
    <p>Don't consider this done just because <code>passwd</code> reported success. After the reboot completes:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Log in as root with the new password at the normal prompt — confirm it actually authenticates, not just that the command ran.</li>
      <li>Run <code>getenforce</code> to confirm SELinux came back in the state you expect (usually <code>Enforcing</code>).</li>
      <li>Check for fresh AVC denials after doing normal work, in case the relabel missed something: <code>ausearch -m AVC -ts recent</code>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Close the gap: security best practices after a reset</h3>
    <p>
      This procedure is also its biggest weakness: anyone who can reach
      the console can reset root the same way, with no password required
      to get there. That's by design — it's a recovery path, not a bug —
      but it means console access to the machine <em>is</em> root access.
      Once you're back in, close that gap:
    </p>
    <pre><code>grub2-setpassword</code></pre>
    <p>
      This requires a username and password before anyone can edit boot
      parameters at the GRUB menu — the exact step this whole procedure
      depends on. Booting normally still works with no prompt; pressing
      <code>e</code> or <code>c</code> to edit an entry asks for that
      password first. Keep it somewhere your team can recover during a
      real outage, and also <strong>limit console access</strong>
      (KVM/iLO/iDRAC logins, hypervisor console permissions, cloud
      serial-console roles) to people who actually need it — treat a
      console session with the same seriousness as a root shell, because
      after this procedure that's exactly what it grants.
    </p>

    <h2 id="svcaccount">A service account has interactive login it shouldn't have</h2>
    <p>
      An account used only by an application, automation tool, or service
      has <code>/bin/bash</code> as its login shell — meaning it can SSH or
      log in interactively even though nothing about its job requires
      that. This isn't usually a fresh compromise; it's more often an
      account created quickly during setup and never revisited. It still
      matters: service accounts should follow least privilege, and an
      unnecessary interactive shell widens the attack surface and is a
      near-guaranteed finding in a security or compliance review.
    </p>

    <h3>Check the account's configured shell</h3>
    <p>For a local Linux account, inspect the passwd database:</p>
    <pre><code>getent passwd ansible_svc</code></pre>
    <p>Example output — the last field is the login shell:</p>
    <pre><code>ansible_svc:x:1005:1005::/home/ansible_svc:/bin/bash</code></pre>
    <p>You can also check the current shell from inside a session:</p>
    <pre><code>echo "$SHELL"
whoami</code></pre>

    <h3>Disable interactive login</h3>
    <p>If the account genuinely doesn't need an interactive shell, assign <code>nologin</code>:</p>
    <pre><code>sudo usermod -s /sbin/nologin ansible_svc</code></pre>
    <p>On some distributions the path is <code>/usr/sbin/nologin</code> instead — confirm it first, then verify the change took effect:</p>
    <pre><code>command -v nologin
cat /etc/shells
getent passwd ansible_svc</code></pre>

    <h3>Confirm it worked</h3>
    <p>A normal interactive shell attempt should now be rejected:</p>
    <pre><code>su - ansible_svc</code></pre>
    <p>A typical response:</p>
    <pre><code>This account is currently not available.</code></pre>

    <h3>nologin vs. false vs. bash</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Shell</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Typical behavior</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><code>/sbin/nologin</code></td><td>Preferred for accounts that must not receive an interactive login shell.</td><td>Rejects the login and commonly displays an explanatory message.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/bin/false</code></td><td>Immediately exits with a failure status.</td><td>No usable interactive shell; generally less descriptive to the user.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><code>/bin/bash</code></td><td>Normal interactive shell.</td><td>Allows shell access whenever authentication and SSH/PAM policy permit it.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Important AD/SSSD clarification:</strong> for Active
      Directory users, do not assume <code>usermod</code> is the right
      fix. An AD identity is normally managed by AD and exposed to Linux
      through SSSD or another identity provider — its shell may come from
      an AD attribute, an SSSD override, or SSSD configuration itself.
      Useful checks:
      <pre><code>getent passwd 'user@domain.example'
id 'user@domain.example'
grep -E '^(shell_fallback|override_shell)' /etc/sssd/sssd.conf 2&gt;/dev/null</code></pre>
      If SSSD's <code>override_shell</code> is set, it can force a shell
      for domain users regardless of local changes. If only selected AD
      accounts need different behavior, use the identity-management
      method approved for your environment rather than a global change
      with no impact analysis.
    </div>

    <h3>Don't break automation</h3>
    <p>
      Before changing a service account to <code>nologin</code>, verify
      how the application actually uses it. Some automation products
      require SSH command execution or a shell even though no human ever
      logs in interactively — changing the shell without testing can
      break jobs, deployments, backups, file transfers, or scheduled
      automation. Check at least: account purpose, SSH usage, sudo rules,
      cron/systemd jobs, application ownership, scripts, SFTP
      requirements, and any dependency on shell startup files.
    </p>

    <h3>Production hardening workflow</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Action</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>1</td><td>Identify whether the account is human, service, application, or automation.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>2</td><td>Check the effective shell with <code>getent passwd</code>.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>3</td><td>Confirm whether interactive SSH/shell access is actually required.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>4</td><td>For local accounts, change to <code>nologin</code> when appropriate.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>5</td><td>For AD accounts, identify where the shell attribute is controlled before changing it.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>6</td><td>Test application/automation functionality after the change.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>7</td><td>Verify SSH/<code>su</code> behavior and document the hardening change.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Interview-ready answer</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Question:</strong> How would you disable interactive login
      for a Linux service account or an AD-backed account?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer:</strong> First I'd identify whether the account is
      local or directory-managed, and confirm whether the application
      requires shell or SSH execution. For a local service account that
      doesn't need an interactive shell, I'd set its shell to
      <code>/sbin/nologin</code> with <code>usermod -s</code>, then verify
      it with <code>getent passwd</code> and test the application. For an
      AD user, I'd first determine whether the shell comes from AD
      attributes, SSSD configuration, or an override, and change it
      through the appropriate identity-management control rather than
      treating the AD identity like a local account — then test every
      dependent automation before closing the change.
    </div>

    <h3>Key lesson</h3>
    <p>
      The goal isn't simply to replace <code>/bin/bash</code>. The goal is
      to enforce least privilege without breaking the workload: determine
      the account type, understand how it's actually used, apply the
      correct identity control, test the service, and document the
      result.
    </p>

    <h2 id="passwdshadow">Why are /etc/passwd and /etc/shadow separate?</h2>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Short answer:</strong> Linux keeps general account
      information in <code>/etc/passwd</code>, which has to stay readable
      by many programs, while password hashes and password-aging data
      live in the much more restricted <code>/etc/shadow</code>. The
      separation limits exposure of the password hashes specifically,
      without having to lock down account metadata that lots of tools
      legitimately need to read.
    </div>

    <h3>What /etc/passwd contains</h3>
    <p>Basic account information used by the OS and applications. A typical entry:</p>
    <pre><code>satish:x:1001:1001:Satish:/home/satish:/bin/bash</code></pre>
    <p>The fields, in order:</p>
    <pre><code>username : password-placeholder : UID : GID : comment : home-directory : login-shell</code></pre>
    <p>
      The <code>x</code> in the password field normally means the actual
      password hash lives in <code>/etc/shadow</code> instead.
    </p>

    <h3>Why /etc/passwd is readable</h3>
    <p>
      Many system tools and applications need account metadata —
      usernames, UIDs, GIDs, home directories, login shells — so
      <code>/etc/passwd</code> is traditionally readable by all local
      users:
    </p>
    <pre><code>ls -l /etc/passwd
-rw-r--r--. 1 root root ... /etc/passwd</code></pre>
    <p>World-readable does <strong>not</strong> mean world-writable. Ordinary users can read the file but not modify it.</p>

    <h3>What /etc/shadow contains</h3>
    <p>Security-sensitive authentication data, including password hashes and password-aging fields. A conceptual entry:</p>
    <pre><code>satish:$6$...:20000:0:99999:7:::</code></pre>
    <p>
      The password field is a <strong>one-way password hash</strong>, not
      an encrypted password that can simply be decrypted. Modern Linux
      systems may use algorithms like <code>yescrypt</code> or SHA-512
      depending on distribution and policy.
    </p>

    <h3>Why /etc/shadow is restricted</h3>
    <p>
      If unprivileged users could read password hashes, they could copy
      them and run offline password-guessing attacks without ever
      touching the login service — restricting access significantly cuts
      that exposure:
    </p>
    <pre><code>ls -l /etc/shadow</code></pre>
    <p>
      Typical permissions vary by distribution — a root-only mode like
      <code>000</code> or <code>600</code>, or controlled group access
      like <code>root:shadow</code> with restrictive permissions. The
      important point is the same everywhere: ordinary users shouldn't be
      able to read it.
    </p>

    <h3>Compare the two files</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>/etc/passwd</th><th>/etc/shadow</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Purpose</td><td>General account metadata</td><td>Password hashes and aging/security data</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Typical readability</td><td>Readable by ordinary users</td><td>Restricted to privileged access</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Password hash</td><td>Normally not stored here; <code>x</code> placeholder</td><td>Stored here</td></tr>
          <tr><td>User/UID/GID</td><td>Yes</td><td>No general UID/GID mapping</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Home &amp; shell</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Security sensitivity</td><td>Moderate</td><td>High</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>What if password hashes were stored in /etc/passwd?</h3>
    <p>
      Historically, that's exactly where Unix systems stored them. As
      password-cracking capability increased, exposing hashes to every
      local user became a real risk, so shadow-password systems moved the
      sensitive hash data into a restricted file while keeping broad
      access to the non-secret account information — letting programs
      still resolve account info without ordinary users being able to
      simply read the local password-hash database.
    </p>

    <h3>Useful verification commands</h3>
    <pre><code>ls -l /etc/passwd /etc/shadow
stat /etc/passwd /etc/shadow
getent passwd
sudo getent shadow
sudo pwck -r</code></pre>
    <p>
      <code>getent</code> is useful because user information may come
      from local files or an external identity source like LDAP/SSSD,
      not only from <code>/etc/passwd</code> directly.
    </p>

    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Important security clarification:</strong> it's common to
      describe the password as being stored in "encrypted format." The
      more technically accurate wording for an interview:
      <code>/etc/shadow</code> stores password <strong>hashes</strong>.
      Password verification hashes the supplied password with the
      configured scheme and compares the result — the original password
      is never recovered by "decrypting" the stored value, because a hash
      isn't reversible the way encryption is.
    </div>

    <h3>Scenario-based interview questions</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Why are passwd and shadow separate?</td><td>To keep widely needed account metadata readable while restricting password hashes.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Is /etc/passwd world-readable?</td><td>Typically yes, because many programs need account metadata.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Does /etc/passwd contain the password hash?</td><td>Normally no — the <code>x</code> field points conceptually to shadow-based storage.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>What does /etc/shadow store?</td><td>Password hashes plus password-aging/account-expiration fields.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Why protect password hashes?</td><td>Exposure enables offline password-guessing attacks.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Are shadow permissions identical on every distribution?</td><td>No — exact mode/group varies; ordinary users should never have unrestricted read access.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Interview-ready answer</h3>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Question:</strong> Why are <code>/etc/passwd</code> and
      <code>/etc/shadow</code> separate?
      <br /><br />
      <strong>Answer:</strong> They're separated so Linux can make
      general account information available to programs without exposing
      password hashes to ordinary users. <code>/etc/passwd</code>
      contains username, UID, GID, home directory, and login shell, and
      is normally world-readable — its password field usually holds
      <code>x</code>. The actual password hash and password-aging
      information live in the restricted <code>/etc/shadow</code> file.
      If password hashes were readable by every user, an attacker with
      local access could copy them and run offline password-guessing
      attacks. So the shadow-password design separates commonly needed
      identity metadata from sensitive authentication data.
    </div>

    <h3>Key lesson</h3>
    <p>
      A strong answer explains not just that the files are separated
      "for security," but <em>why</em>: account metadata needs broad read
      access, password hashes do not. The separation preserves system
      usability while cutting credential-hash exposure to the accounts
      that actually need it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="selinux">SELinux troubleshooting: when permissions look right but access is still denied</h2>
    <p>
      A specific, very common variant of "everything looks fine but the
      application still gets Permission Denied": <code>chmod 777</code>
      changes discretionary access control (DAC) — the traditional
      owner/group/mode permissions — but on RHEL, CentOS, Fedora, and
      other SELinux-enabled distributions, mandatory access control (MAC)
      is a second, independent gate. SELinux can still deny an operation
      that DAC permissions would otherwise allow. Click a question below
      to jump straight to that scenario.
    </p>

    <div class="qa-accordion">
      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>1. An application gets "Permission Denied" even after chmod 777. What should you check first?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Do not assume normal Linux permissions are the only cause. Check SELinux, especially when it is in <strong>Enforcing</strong> mode. SELinux can deny an operation even when owner/group/mode permissions allow it.</p>
          <pre><code>getenforce
sestatus</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>2. Why can chmod 777 still result in Permission Denied?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p><code>chmod 777</code> changes discretionary access control (DAC) permissions only. SELinux uses mandatory access control (MAC) based on security contexts and policy. If the process type is not allowed to access the target file type, SELinux can still block the request.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>3. How do you check whether disk space is causing the problem?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Use <code>df -h</code> for filesystem capacity and <code>df -i</code> for inode usage. If both have sufficient free space, continue with permissions, ownership, SELinux, mount options, and application logs.</p>
          <pre><code>df -h
df -i</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>4. What is the most useful place to check SELinux denials?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>SELinux denials are normally recorded as AVC messages in the Linux audit log. Review recent AVC events instead of immediately disabling SELinux.</p>
          <pre><code>ausearch -m AVC -ts recent
grep -i 'avc:.*denied' /var/log/audit/audit.log</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>5. What does AVC mean in SELinux troubleshooting?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>AVC stands for <strong>Access Vector Cache</strong>. An AVC denial records an SELinux policy decision that denied an attempted action. It can show the source context, target context, object class, requested permission, process, path, and timestamp.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>6. What important fields should you examine in an AVC denial?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Focus on the denied operation and these fields: <code>scontext</code> (source/process context), <code>tcontext</code> (target context), <code>tclass</code> (object class), the requested permission such as write/create/open, the process name, the target path, and the timestamp.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>7. How can you check the SELinux context of a file or directory?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Use <code>ls -Z</code>. For a directory and its contents, <code>ls -ldZ</code> and <code>ls -lZ</code> are useful. Compare the context with a known-good directory used by the same application.</p>
          <pre><code>ls -ldZ /path/to/directory
ls -lZ /path/to/directory</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>8. What command can restore the expected SELinux context?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Use <code>restorecon</code> when the correct file-context rule already exists. The recursive verbose form is commonly used for a directory tree.</p>
          <pre><code>restorecon -Rv /path/to/directory</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>9. Why is restorecon better than chcon for a permanent fix?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p><code>restorecon</code> applies the context defined by SELinux file-context policy. A context set only with <code>chcon</code> can be lost after relabeling or a later <code>restorecon</code>. For a custom path, define a persistent mapping with <code>semanage fcontext</code> and then run <code>restorecon</code>.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>10. How do you permanently assign an SELinux type to a custom application directory?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Create a persistent file-context rule using <code>semanage fcontext</code>, then apply it with <code>restorecon</code>. The exact SELinux type must match the application requirement.</p>
          <pre><code>semanage fcontext -a -t &lt;required_type&gt; '/custom/path(/.*)?'
restorecon -Rv /custom/path</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>11. Should you disable SELinux to solve the issue?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Normally, no. Disabling SELinux removes an important security control and can hide the actual configuration problem. Use audit evidence to correct the label, boolean, policy, or application configuration instead.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>12. Is setenforce 0 a valid troubleshooting test?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>It can be used as a short, controlled diagnostic test where security policy permits it. If the operation works in Permissive mode but fails in Enforcing mode, SELinux is strongly implicated. Re-enable Enforcing immediately and fix the underlying issue.</p>
          <pre><code>setenforce 0
# reproduce the issue
setenforce 1</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>13. How do you check SELinux booleans that may affect a web application?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>List relevant booleans and inspect those associated with the service. Enable a boolean only when the application genuinely requires that behavior.</p>
          <pre><code>getsebool -a | grep -i httpd
setsebool -P &lt;boolean_name&gt; on</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>14. What is a safe production troubleshooting sequence?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Check application/service logs; verify filesystem space and inodes; verify owner/group/mode and ACLs; check SELinux mode; search recent AVC denials; inspect source and target contexts; validate expected policy; apply the smallest correct fix; reproduce the operation; confirm new AVC denials no longer appear; and document the change.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>15. After applying the SELinux fix, how do you validate it?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Repeat the exact failed application action, verify that it succeeds, check application logs, and search the audit log again for fresh AVC denials. Confirm that SELinux remains Enforcing.</p>
          <pre><code>getenforce
ausearch -m AVC -ts recent</code></pre>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>16. What is the difference between DAC and MAC in this scenario?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>DAC is the traditional Linux permission model controlled through ownership, mode bits, and ACLs. SELinux adds MAC, where policy controls whether a labeled process can access a labeled object. Both checks must allow the operation.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>17. A web server can read a directory but cannot write to it. What would you investigate?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Check Unix write permissions and ACLs, filesystem read-only status, available space/inodes, and SELinux AVC denials. For SELinux, confirm whether the directory has a type the web-service domain is permitted to write to and whether a relevant boolean is required.</p>
        </div>
      </details>

      <details class="qa-item">
        <summary>18. What should you avoid when creating a custom SELinux policy?</summary>
        <div class="qa-answer">
          <p>Do not automatically allow every denial produced by a policy-generation tool. First confirm the action is legitimate and that the problem is not simply an incorrect label or an existing SELinux boolean. Grant only the minimum required access.</p>
        </div>
      </details>
    </div>

    <h3>Quick command reference</h3>
    <div class="ref-table-wrap">
      <table class="ref-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Purpose</th><th>Command</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Check SELinux mode</td><td><code>getenforce</code> / <code>sestatus</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Recent SELinux denials</td><td><code>ausearch -m AVC -ts recent</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Audit log search</td><td><code>grep -i 'avc:.*denied' /var/log/audit/audit.log</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Check SELinux labels</td><td><code>ls -ldZ /path</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Restore expected labels</td><td><code>restorecon -Rv /path</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Persistent custom mapping</td><td><code>semanage fcontext -a -t &lt;type&gt; '/path(/.*)?'</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Filesystem usage</td><td><code>df -h</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Inode usage</td><td><code>df -i</code></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Relevant booleans</td><td><code>getsebool -a | grep -i httpd</code></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="callout">
      <strong>Production note:</strong> exact SELinux types and booleans
      depend on the application and path. Validate AVC evidence before
      making a persistent policy change.
    </div>

    <h2 id="habits">Build the habit: capture evidence, not just fixes</h2>
    <p>
      The single biggest difference between a team that firefights forever
      and one that gets calmer over time is whether they write down what
      actually happened. "Reboot fixed it" is a recovery action, not a
      root cause — if you can't explain the trigger, say so explicitly and
      add the logging or monitoring that would catch it next time.
    </p>
    <p>
      A short, honest incident note — what broke, what you found, what you
      changed, what you'd add to catch it earlier — is worth more than a
      perfect fix with no record of how you got there.
    </p>

